Package: pad-osk
Version: 1.1.0
Architecture: all
Maintainer: erhai <user@opd2413.local>
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Depends: gnome-shell (>= 45), gnome-shell (<< 60), libglib2.0-bin
Recommends: ibus, ibus-libpinyin
Description: On-screen keyboard with palettes, sizes and transparency
 A GNOME Shell extension that draws a PC-style on-screen keyboard and injects
 keys through Clutter.VirtualInputDevice, inside the compositor. Tested on
 the OnePlus Pad 2 Pro; any device running GNOME 50 Wayland should work.
 The interface is translated (English source, Simplified Chinese included)
 and follows the system language.
 .
 This is the only approach that works on GNOME Wayland: normal windows cannot
 accept a click without stealing keyboard focus, and mutter refuses external
 input injection (no zwp_virtual_keyboard, no wlr-layer-shell). Because the keys
 are injected as real input events, apps that never speak zwp_text_input_v3 --
 Chromium and Electron (Chrome, QQ) -- receive them normally.
 .
 Layout is PC-style with a number row, sticky modifiers, arrow keys and a
 Chinese/English input-method toggle. Every character key, the erase key and
 the arrows repeat while held. The gear on the grip bar opens a settings card
 of its own: it drops below (or above) the keyboard, titles itself, and lists
 one row per setting in the MIUIX style -- icon, title and subtitle, the
 current value and a chevron that expands the row. The rows pick one of five
 key sizes (55%, 62%, 66%, 71%, 76% of the screen width), the palette
 (graphite / light / warm / high contrast, plus a custom palette with presets,
 a spectrum and a hex field for panel, key, modifier, pressed and accent),
 how see-through the panel is (100% down to 50%), the corner radius, and an
 optional backdrop image from ~/.config/pad-osk/backgrounds/. The About group
 carries a reset button, a link to the project site and a link to the GitHub
 releases. Settings are remembered per user and can be fine-tuned from
 ~/.config/pad-osk/position.kf. The panel can be dragged, collapsed to a thin
 bar, closed, and hidden from Quick Settings or the application grid. It steps
 aside when the shutdown/session dialog appears.
